  "name": "Question 1",
  "problem": "In **ascending order,** the solutions of the equation $2{x}^{2}\\cdot {7}^{2x}-50\\cdot {7}^{2x}=0$ are\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}$\\,$ and $\\,x=$\n{{field-2}}.",
  "worked_solution": "First, we factor the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}\\cdot {7}^{2x}-50\\cdot {7}^{2x} & =0 \\\\ (2{x}^{2}-50)\\cdot {7}^{2x} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the zero product property, which states that $ab=0$ if and only if $a=0$ or $b=0.$ This gives us two equations,\n\n$$\n2{x}^{2}-50=0\\,\\text{and}\\,{7}^{2x}=0.\n$$\n\nWe now solve each of these equations separately.\n\n- The equation $2{x}^{2}-50=0$ can be factored as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2({x}^{2}-25) & =0 \\\\ 2(x-5)(x+5) & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich leads to the solutions $x=-5,5.$\n\n- The equation $2=0$ is not a possible solution because it's a false statement.\n\n- The equation ${7}^{2x}=0$ has no solutions because ${7}^{2x}$ is positive for every real number $x.$\n\nTherefore, in ascending order, the original equation has the solutions \n\n$$\nx=-5\\,\\text{and}\\,x=5.\n$$",
